# ABSTRACT

The accumulation of floating solid waste in water bodies is an environmental concern that affects aquatic ecosystems, water quality and the cleanliness of natural and artificial water environments. Conventional waste collection methods often require manual intervention and may be difficult to perform continuously in certain locations.

This project presents NEER, a low-cost floating water waste collection robot designed to retrieve floating solid waste from the surface of water bodies. The system integrates a buoyant mechanical structure, motor-driven propulsion, wireless control and a rotating mesh-based waste collection mechanism.

An ESP32 microcontroller serves as the central processing unit. Wireless communication is implemented through Wi-Fi using an HTTP-based web server, allowing an operator to issue movement commands remotely. Geared DC motors provide propulsion, while a relay-controlled collection mechanism operates the motor responsible for the mechanical waste retrieval assembly.

The robot incorporates a lightweight supporting structure, floating materials and a dedicated waste collection compartment. The design aims to demonstrate the feasibility of combining affordable embedded electronics and mechanical engineering to develop a compact water surface cleaning platform.

The prototype is evaluated through flotation, stability, wireless communication, propulsion and waste collection tests. The project also identifies possible improvements, including autonomous navigation, obstacle detection, sensor integration and enhanced collection efficiency.

**Keywords:** Floating Robot, Water Waste Collection, ESP32, Robotics, Wi-Fi Control, Conveyor Mechanism, Environmental Sustainability.

