# CHAPTER 1: INTRODUCTION

## 1.1 Background

Water bodies are important environmental resources that support aquatic ecosystems, human activities and biodiversity. However, the accumulation of floating solid waste has become a persistent problem in many water environments.

Plastic wrappers, packaging materials, bottles, leaves and other floating debris can accumulate on water surfaces and affect the cleanliness and ecological condition of these environments.

Regular removal of such waste is necessary. However, manual collection can be repetitive, labour-intensive and difficult to carry out continuously.

Advances in embedded systems, robotics and wireless communication provide opportunities to develop compact robotic systems that can assist in environmental maintenance.

NEER is developed around this concept. It combines a floating platform with motor-driven navigation and a mechanical waste collection mechanism.

## 1.2 Problem Statement

The conventional collection of floating waste requires manual effort and may involve considerable time and labour.

Small water bodies and restricted water environments may not justify the use of large cleaning equipment. There is therefore a need to explore affordable, compact and accessible alternatives.

The project addresses this problem through the development of a floating robotic platform that can be remotely controlled and equipped with a mechanical system for collecting floating debris.

## 1.3 Aim

To design and develop a low-cost floating robotic system capable of navigating the surface of water and collecting floating solid waste through a motor-driven mechanical mechanism.

## 1.4 Objectives

1. Develop a lightweight floating platform.
2. Integrate a motor-driven propulsion system.
3. Implement wireless control through an ESP32 microcontroller.
4. Develop a mechanical mechanism for floating waste collection.
5. Provide a dedicated compartment for storing collected waste.
6. Integrate the electronic and mechanical subsystems.
7. Evaluate the prototype through controlled experiments.
8. Identify improvements for future development.

## 1.5 Scope

The current project focuses on a prototype for floating surface waste collection.

The scope includes:
- Floating structure fabrication.
- Motor-driven propulsion.
- ESP32-based wireless control.
- Mechanical waste collection.
- Waste storage.
- Basic system integration and testing.

The current implementation does not establish autonomous navigation, automatic waste recognition or underwater waste collection.

## 1.6 Significance

The project demonstrates the application of robotics and embedded electronics to an environmental problem.

It provides an opportunity to explore how low-cost hardware, mechanical design and wireless control can be integrated into a functional engineering prototype.

